I included the FLASH comments because @ioannisg mentioned flash partitioning and I wasn't clear on the Linux binding. It does have some relevance in that we need to partition both FLASH and RAM somehow, and the closer the two solutions are to each other the more we can share in the implementation.

> On this point I didn't have any better idea and I'm open to any suggestion

As I hint at in the "one partitioning method" comment, I think the only robust solution is to reduce the size of the `SRAM` region so that it doesn't overlap reserved memory (via `reserved-memory` or any other solution). This requires being explicit in how large application RAM should be, rather than the current implicit situation. This also links in to the partition comment below.

> What's the difference? Only the davicetree location?

By directly hanging the partitions under a devicetree node, you could do different things based on whether a partition is defined at all. If for example you had:
```
		sram2:memory@20020000 {
			compatible = "mmio-sram";
			reg = <0x20020000 DT_SIZE_K(32)>;
		};
```
You would generate linker region `sram2`, but with:
```
		sram2:memory@20020000 {
			compatible = "mmio-sram";
			reg = <0x20020000 DT_SIZE_K(32)>;
			parititions {
				compatible = "ram-partitions";
				app: partition@{...};
				lib1: partition@{...};
				lib2: partition@{...};
			};
		};
```
You would generate `app`, `lib1` and `lib2` instead. I am assuming that the following statements are true:
* RAM devicetree nodes should result in one or more linker memory regions
* If part of a RAM block is 'reserved', that memory range should not exist in other memory regions

I can't really see a way to do this if all 'partitions' live under `reserved-memory` (if the described behavior is desired).

> I included the FLASH comments because @ioannisg mentioned flash partitioning and I wasn't clear on the Linux binding. It does have some relevance in that we need to partition both FLASH and RAM somehow, and the closer the two solutions are to each other the more we can share in the implementation.

Agree, if we can find some common ground I'll be happy to share the implementation :)

> As I hint at in the "one partitioning method" comment, I think the only robust solution is to reduce the size of the `SRAM` region so that it doesn't overlap reserved memory (via `reserved-memory` or any other solution). This requires being explicit in how large application RAM should be, rather than the current implicit situation. This also links in to the partition comment below.

Yes, I thought about this but that would be really unpleasant to manage if we would like for example to reserve one single page in the middle of the SRAM region, because you would end up with something like:
```
MEMORY {
  SRAM1 (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40000000, LENGTH = (XXX * 1K)
  RES: (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40040000, LENGTH = (4 * 1K)
  SRAM2 (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40041000, LENGTH = (YYY * 1K)
}
```
The real problem is that sometimes you are not really free to decide whether your reserved memory should be placed in memory. Think about some kind of shared memory between several SoCs on the same platform to be placed at some very specific address in memory that could end up being in the middle of some region.

> By directly hanging the partitions under a devicetree node, you could do different things based on whether a partition is defined at all. If for example you had:
> 
> ```
> 		sram2:memory@20020000 {
> 			compatible = "mmio-sram";
> 			reg = <0x20020000 DT_SIZE_K(32)>;
> 		};
> ```
> 
> You would generate linker region `sram2`, but with:
> 
> ```
> 		sram2:memory@20020000 {
> 			compatible = "mmio-sram";
> 			reg = <0x20020000 DT_SIZE_K(32)>;
> 			parititions {
> 				compatible = "ram-partitions";
> 				app: partition@{...};
> 				lib1: partition@{...};
> 				lib2: partition@{...};
> 			};
> 		};
> ```
> 
> You would generate `app`, `lib1` and `lib2` instead.

That would be really inconvenient in the case I mentioned before. Think for example if I need to reserve two buffers with some particular cache-ability attributes to use as memory pools. If I understand correctly your point I should do something like:
```
	sram2:memory@20020000 {
 			compatible = "mmio-sram";
 			reg = <0x20020000 DT_SIZE_K(32)>;
 			parititions {
 				compatible = "ram-partitions";
                                sram_beginning: partition@{...};
                                buf1: partition@{...};
                                sram_middle : partition@{...};
                                buf2: partition@{...};
                                sram_end: partition@{...};
 			};
 		};
```
This looks painful to me. I should be able to just define `buf1` and `buf2` under `reserved-memory` node and the code should take care of all the details for me.

> I am assuming that the following statements are true:
> 
>     * RAM devicetree nodes should result in one or more linker memory regions
> 
>     * If part of a RAM block is 'reserved', that memory range should not exist in other memory regions
> 
> 
> I can't really see a way to do this if all 'partitions' live under `reserved-memory` (if the described behavior is desired).

Also these statements are not exactly true because RAM devicetree nodes could not necessarily result in one or more linker regions. What we need are sections and possibly linker script symbols to mark start and end of those sections. For example instead of having something like this (generated by the current PR):
```
MEMORY {
  RES0 (rw) : ORIGIN = 0x40000000, LENGTH = 0x1000
}
...
res0 : {
  _reserved_res0_start = .
  ...
  _reserved_res0_end = _reserved_res0_start + 0x1000;
} > RES0
```
You could have something like:
```
res0 0x40000000 : {
  _reserved_res0_start = .
  ...
  _reserved_res0_end = _reserved_res0_start + 0x1000;
}
```
So I'm adding the memory regions just for clarity but the main work here is done by the sections. This is probably not important for this discussion but I just wanted to put this on the table as well.

Now, there are two more things I don't like about your proposal of having partitions as child-nodes of `mmio-sram` nodes:
1. This is not the standard way to define `reserved-memory` nodes. Like it or not Linux has already defined a way to do that with a standalone node. We could diverge from that but I don't think that would bring many advantages.

2. I think that it is conceptually wrong to think the reserved-memory regions as "belonging" to some other memory region. You are not partitioning anything here, you are defining an entirely new section for some peculiar use.

> Yes, I thought about this but that would be really unpleasant to manage if we would like for example to reserve one single page in the middle of the SRAM region, because you would end up with something like:

I agree that such a situation is unpleasant to manage, but how is such a situation handled using `reserved-memory`?
```
MEMORY {
  SRAM (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40000000, LENGTH = (1M)
  RES: (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40001000, LENGTH = (4 * 1K)
}
```
So  you have a two memory regions, one of which lies over the other.
In terms of the linker script, this results in something like:
```
{
	__data_ram_start = .;
	*(.data)
	*(".data.*")
	__data_ram_end = .;
} > GROUP_LINK_IN(SRAM)

{
	__res_start = .;
	*(.res)
	*(".res.*")
	__res_end = .;
} > GROUP_LINK_IN(RES)
```
The first 4K of `.data` is fine, no issues. But as soon as you go past 4K, you're overriding the reserved memory.
More importantly you have no way of placing variables **after** the memory region you've reserved.

> The real problem is that sometimes you are not really free to decide whether your reserved memory should be placed in memory. Think about some kind of shared memory between several SoCs on the same platform to be placed at some very specific address in memory that could end up being in the middle of some region.

Are there examples of this situation where reserved memory sits in the middle of the main SRAM address space?

> This looks painful to me. I should be able to just define buf1 and buf2 under reserved-memory node and the code should take care of all the details for me.

I agree it is painful, but I believe that is the price that must be paid to ensure all RAM is usable. `reserved-memory` doesn't take care of all the details for you, it just stops you from being able to use any memory after the first `reserved-memory` address. This was one of the major problems with my first PR on this topic, see https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/31077#issuecomment-760770572 (albeit for flash rather than ram).

> You are not partitioning anything here, you are defining an entirely new section for some peculiar use.

I disagree with this, by definition you are partitioning an existing address space into multiple address spaces by saying that these addresses are reserved. You're not magically creating some new memory addresses that didn't previously exist.

> I agree that such a situation is unpleasant to manage, but how is such a situation handled using `reserved-memory`?
> 
> ```
> MEMORY {
>   SRAM (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40000000, LENGTH = (1M)
>   RES: (wx) : ORIGIN = 0x40001000, LENGTH = (4 * 1K)
> }
> ```
> 
> So you have a two memory regions, one of which lies over the other.
> In terms of the linker script, this results in something like:
> 
> ```
> {
> 	__data_ram_start = .;
> 	*(.data)
> 	*(".data.*")
> 	__data_ram_end = .;
> } > GROUP_LINK_IN(SRAM)
> 
> {
> 	__res_start = .;
> 	*(.res)
> 	*(".res.*")
> 	__res_end = .;
> } > GROUP_LINK_IN(RES)
> ```
> 
> The first 4K of `.data` is fine, no issues. But as soon as you go past 4K, you're overriding the reserved memory.

Yes, you are in theory, but you are not going to ever find yourself in this situation because the compiler would stop you at compile time if any section is overlapping with each other (again, assuming that you have some kind of variable in `.res` to fill up the section).

Let's take a real example. Let's say that I want to have a memory pool for DMA and I want to be able to configure this memory pool using MPU so that it has some special no-cache attribute. To be able to do this I'd like to reserved some memory somewhere at a know address so that I can use this address in the MPU code to set the cache-ability attribute and use the same address for the slab allocator. This is a real case where I don't care about partitioning or memory regions, I just want to be able to precisely locate a buffer in memory using a section.

Now, let's take the `qemu_cortex_a53` board, just because I'm familiar with that and see how the `hello_world` sample is organizing the memory in this case.

Looking at the map file, everything is basically in different sections between the start and end symbols:
```
0x0000000040000000                _image_text_start = .
0x0000000040000000                z_mapped_start = .
... {a lot of sections here}
0x000000004005b000                _image_ram_end = .
0x000000004005b000                _end = .
0x000000004005b000                z_mapped_end = .
0x0000000048000000                __kernel_ram_end = 0x48000000
```
Now, what I would do in this case is to have something like:
```
reserved-memory {
    compatible = "reserved-memory";

    reserved: reserved@42000000 {
        reg = <0x42000000 0x8000>;
        label = "reserved";
        linker-section;
        non-cacheable;
    };
};  
```
So that I can create at `0x42000000` a section that I can use to place the buffer to be used as memory pool. This section is not overlapping with any other section and if it overlapped, we would have got a complain from the linker already.

> More importantly you have no way of placing variables **after** the memory region you've reserved.

I'm not sure I follow you here, sorry. As in the example reserved regions should be located far from the image sections, possibly at the end, otherwise as stated before you would get a linker error. If we get no linker error we can be pretty sure that no Zephyr variable is going to be allocated there (pretty much everything in Zephyr is inside some section).

> Are there examples of this situation where reserved memory sits in the middle of the main SRAM address space?

Maybe not in the middle, but the point is that you still have to be able to point to a specific region in memory and have exclusive access to that. Doing that having a section to a very precise memory address looks to me like a good idea.

