@ceolin @nashif The rationale behind _pm actions_:

If we take Linux as a reference, all PM operations are held by `struct dev_pm_ops`:
```c
struct dev_pm_ops {
	...
	int (*prepare)(struct device *dev);
	void (*complete)(struct device *dev);
	int (*suspend)(struct device *dev);
	int (*resume)(struct device *dev);
        ...
};
```
ref https://elixir.bootlin.com/linux/latest/source/include/linux/pm.h#L277

As you can see, each callback represents an _action_: _prepare_, _suspend_... States are not reflected here. The proposal for Zephyr is to have a single callback (as we do now) with the action as an argument, that is:
```c
int my_pm_control(const struct device *dev, enum pm_device_action action);
```

When drawing a device state diagram, actions become the natural way to represent transitions:

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/25011557/125063229-83332d80-e0af-11eb-970e-c34890c77742.png)

If considering async case:
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/25011557/125063410-baa1da00-e0af-11eb-8d54-ca0959d09dcb.png)

