> Thanks for sharing @nandojve. How does the SoC handle low power modes? Is it done automatically by the peripheral?

In case of Cypress, peripheral is complete disconnected from pins. I mean, for each pin use case the pin must be configured. So, it is necessary case to achieve low power consumption.

I think the main problematic is that there are SoCs that handle on the peripheral itself and there are other SoCs that require a pin state change to achieve the lowest power consumption. Zephyr should handle both situations.

> FYI 1cf34dc adds a quick proof of concept on how runtime pinctrl could work together with a usable example.

What you say it is pincfgs at below:

```
const struct pinctrl_state uart1_cfg0_sleep = {
	.name = "sleep",
	.pincfgs = (const uint32_t[]){
		PINCTRL_NRF(0, 6, INP, IDISC, PN, UART_TX),
		PINCTRL_NRF(0, 8, INP, IDISC, PN, UART_RX),
	},
	.pincfgs_cnt = 2U,
};
```

is in fact, at current Zephyr implementation like a gpio c structure. See below:

This
`DT_CYPRESS_PIN(uart5, rx, 5, 0, act_6, bias-pull-up, input-enable); `

auto generate this
```
p5_0_uart5_rx: p5_0_uart5_rx { 
   cypress,pins = <&gpio_prt5 0x0 0x12 >; 
   bias-pull-up; 
   input-enable; 
} 

```
which auto generate
https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/dc633856d706a9e78869122260305fb2fd5c68d1/soc/arm/cypress/common/soc_gpio.h#L67-L71

and currently it is used by drivers
https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/dc633856d706a9e78869122260305fb2fd5c68d1/drivers/serial/uart_psoc6.c#L53-L54

Now, you add a very interesting point related to how to group it. What I would like know is if we can store properties from [pincfg-node](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/6f4f1dc2302af06c92c560562d4d499666c6831a/dts/bindings/pinctrl/pincfg-node.yaml) at pin struct, instead on a group state C structure.

Example, below constructions could be equivalent at c structure:

```
n: pin_group_1 {
	pins = <
                     PINCTRL_NRF(0, 8, INP, IDISC, PN, UART_RX
                     PINCTRL_NRF(0, 7, INP, ICONN, PU, UART_CTS)
                   >;

	bias-pull-up; 
	input-enable; 
}

p5_0_uart5_rx: p5_0_uart5_rx { 
   cypress,pins = <&gpio_prt5 0x0 0x12 >; 
   bias-pull-up; 
   input-enable; 
} 

n: pin_group_2 {
	pins = <&p5_0_uart5_rx>;
}
```

In this case, pin_group_1, each pin will receive 2 flag properties **bias-pull-up** and **input-enable** at pin C structure. At pin_group_2 will happen the same. If we can automatically pick properties on group and propagate to each pin I think we can have a solution that may solve low power for any SoC and share API between gpio and pinctrl.

If we will do this on a centralized way (at some pinctrl manager) or distributed (on each driver) it is another thing.

STM32 HAL headers define their own `SCB_CFSR` fields and these are in conflict with the updated/corrected CMSIS definitions.
```
/var/lib/buildkite-agent/zephyr-module-cache/modules/hal/stm32/stm32cube/stm32l1xx/soc/stm32l151xba.h:7797: error: "SCB_CFSR_MMARVALID_Pos" redefined [-Werror]
 7797 | #define SCB_CFSR_MMARVALID_Pos             (SCB_SHCSR_MEMFAULTACT_Pos + 7U)    /*!< SCB CFSR (MMFSR): MMARVALID Position */
      |
In file included from /var/lib/buildkite-agent/zephyr-module-cache/modules/hal/stm32/stm32cube/stm32l1xx/soc/stm32l151xba.h:133,
                 from /var/lib/buildkite-agent/zephyr-module-cache/modules/hal/stm32/stm32cube/stm32l1xx/soc/stm32l1xx.h:131,
                 from ../../../../../../soc/arm/st_stm32/stm32l1/soc.h:22,
                 from ../../../../../../include/arch/arm/aarch32/cortex_m/cmsis.h:17,
                 from ../../../../../../include/arch/arm/aarch32/mpu/arm_mpu_v7m.h:10,
                 from ../../../../../../include/arch/arm/aarch32/mpu/arm_mpu.h:13,
                 from ../../../../../../include/arch/arm/aarch32/arch.h:187,
                 from ../../../../../../include/arch/cpu.h:19,
                 from ../../../../../../include/kernel_includes.h:33,
                 from ../../../../../../include/kernel.h:17,
                 from /workdir/zephyr/arch/arm/core/offsets/offsets_aarch32.c:28,
                 from /workdir/zephyr/arch/arm/core/offsets/offsets.c:9:
```

The duplicate definitions, as long as they are identical, are not problem; but, here the [new CMSIS release fixed incorrect definitions](https://github.com/ARM-software/CMSIS_5/commit/2e1a5d13866f7318aa5905b7fc8de69382cbd804) and these no longer match the definitions provided by the STM32 HAL (the latter still having incorrect definitions for these).

The incorrect STM32 HAL definitions should be updated accordingly to fix this issue (PR https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/hal_stm32/pull/113).

