Proposed API public names for structs and functions:

```
/* Stores the config for a pin (mapping and other settings such as pull-up */
pinctrl_soc_pin_config_t

/* Stores the config for a state (id, list of pins) */
struct pinctrl_state_config

/* Stores the config for a device (states, register) */
struct pinctrl_{states,dev,func}_config

/* Find a state within device config given a state identifier */
int pinctrl_lookup_state(const struct pinctrl_dev_config *config, uint8_t id,
             const struct pinctrl_state_config **state);

/* Configure a set of pins. This is the function with lowest level access. Only function implemented by pinctrl driver */
int pinctrl_configure_pins(const pinctrl_pin_config_t *pins,
                   uint8_t pins_cnt, uintptr_t reg);

/* Configure a state given its configuration. NOTE: pinctrl_state_select_raw could also be considered */
static inline int pinctrl_apply_state_direct(const struct pinctrl_dev_config *config,
                       const struct pinctrl_state_config *state);

/* Select/apply state from the device config given an ID */
static inline int pinctrl_apply_state(const struct pinctrl_dev_config *config,
                       uint8_t state_id);

int pinctrl_update_states(struct pinctrl_dev_config *config,
			     const struct pinctrl_state_config *states,
			     uint8_t state_cnt);
```

Sorry for necroposting, but I just ran into this for the first time while reading through the Bluetooth guides.

@jhedberg @joerchan why is all the "getting started guide" information duplicated here? It's going to go stale and cause confusion.

I think it would have been better to structure this document assuming the user has already gone through the getting started guide, maybe referencing it if necessary. But all of the "first, apt-get this, then install west, then west init, ..." information is already in the GSG.

The "for production" production approach I will suggest is to add a `fem` property to the devicetree [nordic,nrf-radio](https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/reference/devicetree/bindings/net/wireless/nordic%2Cnrf-radio.html#dtbinding-nordic-nrf-radio) nodes, and depending on its compatible we can decide if it's a simple two-pin PA/LNA device or something more complicated like the nRF21540. That will let us get the PDN and CSN pin handling into DT as follow-up work.

Users will therefore set up a PA/LNA FEM with an overlay that looks roughly like this:

```
my_fem: fem {
        compatible = "generic-fem-two-ctrl-pins";
        pa-gpios = <...>;
        lna-gpios = <...>;
};

&radio {
        fem = <&my_fem>;
};
```

And similarly for an nRF21540, except `my_fem` will have compatible `"nordic,nrf21540-fem"` and will contain different properties.

Then we'll have to decide what to do with the remaining Kconfig options like and CONFIG_BT_CTLR_GPIO_PA CONFIG_BT_CTLR_GPIO_LNA. In principle we won't need them as we could just get everything from devicetree, but it might make sense to keep them if you want to be able to turn the configuration on and off in software. Let me know what you think.

