I'm still not loving that bit API.  It's just a lot of complexity (both for the user and internally) for not a lot of actual expressive space.  And it tends to use words that deal with the internal implementation; e.g. overwrite/accumulate instead of flagging "some" events or setting "all" of them.  And what PRE/POST_CLEAR reflect isn't just ordering of operations but whether or not the call honors pre-existing state (I call that "reset" below).

So one last try at an alternative: Split the functions on one of your boolean axes and pass the other as a straight argument, e.g.:

(Again, I'm not going to be sticky on this if the desire of the community really is for the bit API.  I'm just saying I don't like it and think this would be better.)

```c
/* Waits for any event.  "reset" (the equivalent of PRE_CLEAR)
 * indicates that the event should be cleared atomically before
 * waiting.  Otherwise the existing event state is preserved and this
 * function can be used to "poll" for the state.
 */
uint32_t k_event_wait(struct k_event *event, uint32_t events_mask,
                      bool reset, k_timeout_t timeout);

/* As above, but waits for all events in the mask to be cleared */
uint32_t k_event_wait_all(struct k_event *event, uint32_t events_mask,
                          bool reset, k_timeout_t timeout);


/* As for k_event_post with K_EVENT_ACCUMULATE: signals the events
 * specified in the mask.  Doesn't change the state of unset events.
 * This is what typical code wants to use, almost always.
 */
void k_event_post(struct k_event *event, uint32_t events_mask);

/* Directly sets the post state of all events at once.  Use this to
 * atomically modify more than one event at a time.  Equivalent to
 * K_EVENT_OVERWRITE.
 */
void k_event_set(struct k_event *event, uint32_t events_mask);
```

