> I completely understand the differences, I'm asking how you are using this state right now, since it is not used by the kernel nor device runtime. Another argument is, some devices can have multiple low power states and they will need to handle it internally, that is similar if STATE_OFF is removed.

Ah, basically I have an additional API which handles power domains, as Zephyr obviously doesn't yet.
The requirement I enforce is that my API is called before `device_pm_get`, and optionally after `device_pm_put`.
For example.
```
const struct device *accelerometer = DEVICE_DT_GET(DT_INST(0, blah));

device_req_power_domain(accelerometer);

device_pm_get(accelerometer);

/* Do things */

device_pm_put(accelerometer);

/* Optional depending on whether you want the accelerometer to remain powered */
device_rel_power_domain(accelerometer);
```
Where the `power_domain` functions iterate over supported devices and call `pm_device_state_set` with `PM_DEVICE_STATE_OFF` and `PM_DEVICE_STATE_LOW_POWER` directly. They are implemented over the `onoff_manager` API, so they are implicitly thread-safe.

I've done some more testing regarding the watchdog timer change, since after looking back at the reference manual the RT1170 and RT1160 watchdog timers are all different. The WDOG1 and WDOG2 timers both have the same register interfaces, but WDOG2 is "dedicated for secure world purposes and will be activated by TZ software if required". WDOG3 and 4 are CPU watchdog timers, which do not currently have a Zephyr driver. Since WDOG2 is used by trustzone software only, WDOG1 seems like the logical choice.

I tested both DTS files using the watchdog api test (tests/drivers/watchdog/wdt_basic_api) and found that the tests passed if the following watchdog definition was used:
```
wdog1: wdog@40030000 {
		compatible = "nxp,imx-wdog";
		reg = <0x40030000 0xA>;
		status = "disabled";
		interrupts = <112 0>;
		label = "WDOG1";
	};

wdog2: wdog@40034000 {
		compatible = "nxp,imx-wdog";
		reg = <0x40034000 0xA>;
		status = "disabled";
		interrupts = <65 0>;
		label = "WDOG2";
	};
```
However, using the original watchdog definitions:
```
wdog1: wdog@40038000 {
		compatible = "nxp,imx-wdog";
		reg = <0x40038000 0xA>;
		status = "disabled";
		interrupts = <112 0>;
		label = "WDOG1";
	};

wdog2: wdog@40034000 {
		compatible = "nxp,imx-wdog";
		reg = <0x40034000 0xA>;
		status = "disabled";
		interrupts = <65 0>;
		label = "WDOG2";
	};
```
The test cases fail. Since the RT1170 EVK did not have the watchdog enabled in its board DTS, and the watchdog test was not enabled, I believe that the original version of the watchdog definitions was incorrect, and that the updated one in this PR should be used.

> The assumption here is that a potential second CAN interface is used as back-up for the main device.
> I have processes that handle 3 separate CAN networks at the same time.

Actually, no such assumption is made.

The `zephyr` prefix in the `chosen` properties is for Zephyr system-wide settings. Zephyr by itself does not have any applications or subsystems with support for more than one CAN bus controller (neither for redundancy or other use cases). Zephyr by itself does however have the need for determining which CAN controller to use for simple, uni-interface CAN-enabled applications and subsystems. See https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/guides/dts/intro.html#aliases-and-chosen-nodes for further information on aliases and chosen nodes.

I do not foresee us introducing Zephyr in-tree code supporting e.g. three CAN controllers since that is very domain specific (I do, however, foresee us adding CANopen support with support for redundant networks, hence the comment in the commit message).

Out-of-tree applications, on the other hand, are free to define whatever `chosen` or `aliases` properties they need (but they probably should not be using the `zephyr` prefix for this). An out-of-tree application can e.g. specify the following regardless of this change:

```
/ {
     aliases {
             can-primary = &can1;
             can-secondary = &can2;
             can-tertiary = &can0;
     };
};
```

Or, if that's more fitting:

```
/ {
     aliases {
             can-upstream = &can1;
             can-downstream = &can2;
     };
};
```

The main point here, is that no Zephyr _in-tree_ functionality has support for multi-homed CAN, and that if and when we do introduce that, the `chosen` nodes likely need to have more domain-specific names than `zephyr,can-primary`, `zephyr,can-secondary` etc.

@str4t0m The support code looks nice. I have some comments regarding structure:
I think that the support for 1-Wire device, in form of API should be divided into three layers:
1) physical: reset/detect, single bit tx/rx, single byte tx/rx, line level (to check for example if line is kept low);
1a) multi-byte rx and tx and combined tx/rx (something like send one byte of command get N back);
2) Utils, generic protocol specific: scan for devices, get id, skip rom, match rom, and so on; you know the common codes;
3) Drivers for specific devices - these will require specific API, for example to get temperature or to be attached to existing API, in case of EEPROM devices.

The 1-2 should be exposed to users as API, so that a 1-Wire device could be used without driver, directly from software.
The 1) has been divided into 1) and 1a) because multi-byte, or combined operations, would be generally implemented in software; having 1) as separate API allows to add support for devices like the DS2482-100 i2c-1w bridge (https://www.maximintegrated.com/en/products/interface/controllers-expanders/DS2482-100.html#tech-docs), using the same API as the uart or bit-banging implementation.

