I've added the simplest version of a `power-domain` device and a `qemu` test which cycles some dependent devices.
```
/ {
	test_reg_0: test_reg_0 {
		compatible = "power-domain-gpio";
		label = "test-reg-0";
		enable-gpios = <&gpio0 0 0>;
	};

	test_reg_1: test_reg_1 {
		compatible = "power-domain-gpio";
		label = "test-reg-1";
		enable-gpios = <&gpio0 1 0>;
	};

	test_reg_chained: test_reg_chained {
		compatible = "power-domain-gpio";
		label = "test-reg-chained";
		enable-gpios = <&gpio0 2 0>;
		power-domain = <&test_reg_0>;
	};
};
```
Which shows the sequencing operating correctly.
```
START - test_demo
Enabling runtime power management on regulators
Cycling: test-reg-0
I: test-reg-0 is now ON
I: test-reg-chained is OFF and powered
I: test-reg-chained is OFF and not powered
I: test-reg-0 is now OFF and powered
Cycling: test-reg-1
I: test-reg-1 is now ON
I: test-reg-1 is now OFF and powered
Cycling: test-reg-chained
I: test-reg-0 is now ON
I: test-reg-chained is OFF and powered
I: test-reg-chained is now ON
I: test-reg-chained is now OFF and powered
I: test-reg-chained is OFF and not powered
I: test-reg-0 is now OFF and powered
DONE
```
One thing this exercise has exposed is that correctly implementing devices on power domain depends on making an assumption about the state of the domain by default. If the domain is on by default, you need to run `PM_DEVICE_ACTION_TURN_ON` code in your init function, because you are already powered up. If it is off by default you can't , because the device has no power.

I have made the assumption here that all power domains are turned off until explicitly turned on.
Given how the implementation works, it may even make sense for the drivers to depend on `PM_DEVICE_RUNTIME`.

