> Couple of comments. In general, i would like to discuss if it's possible to extend rtio to support:
> 
> * sequence completion callback

Yeah that would be possible, though intent here is to catch the completions in a thread somewhere, even in the MULTITHREADING=N case I'd expect to want to check the completions in the non-ISR context.


> * adding new sqe to active rtio

io_uring supports this, as should rtio, rtio_submit() should be callable multiple times. Really that just requires checking if there's an ongoing callback chain already proceeding or not or perhaps updating when to stop. io_uring actually has a really nice mode of operation where a thread is constantly running to polling and pulling from the queue, so submit() isn't even needed.

TLDR; This should be supported and have a test case, but does not currently. I'll add it to the checklist above.

> * mpsc rtio
> 
> I have few use cases in mind:
> 
> * asynchronous managing of access to i2c/spi device - mpsc rtio would be used for that

Right, this is sort of where things could get complicated and I'm not sure its worth the complications. The same interfaces could be used as they are today but would require a more complex executor that understands there may be multiple contexts submitting. There's almost certainly going to be a code size cost for that and not something everyone need. That's ok though.

The basic thinking for this from me would be each thread still views and has an RTIO context but share a common, multi-queue executor. The common executor can manage I/O priorities globally rather than locally. There's little benefit I feel in sharing the submission/completion queue pair and a lot of complexities in doing so. Does that make sense to you as well?

There is a need, from the iodev, to know when its ready to be used again if it was busy before (a thread using it, another rtio chain, etc). This is something I need to explore more. In some other setups there are async mutexes where... I mean really its a queue of requesters with callbacks attached when the "mutex" is freed. It's not the same, at all, as the existing thread mutex as I understand it.

> * setting up a sequence where you write data to sensor, wait, read from sensor. async notification
>   some code snippet could look like that (using sem to wait):
> 
> ```
> struct k_sem sem;
> struct async_sem asem = { .sem = &sem};
> static rtio_cb cb = { .cb = async_semaphore_cb, .userdata = &asem };
> rtio_sqe *wr_sqe = rtio_acquire(&rtio);
> rtio_sqe *delay_sqe = rtio_acquire(&rtio);
> rtio_sqe *read_sqe = rtio_acquire(&rtio);
> rtio_write_prep(wr_sqe, rtio_i2c, tx_buf, sizeof(tx_buf));
> rtio_delay_prep(delay_seq, &rtio_delay, K_MSEC(10));
> rtio_read_prep(rd_sqe, rtio_i2c, rx_buf, sizeof(rx_buf));
> 
> err = rtio_submit(&rtio, read_sqe, &cb);
> 
> err = k_sem_take(&sem, K_FOREVER);
> ```
> 
> * setting up a sequence where you write data to sensor, wait for gpio change, read from sensor

It's an interesting way to get notified of completion. Maybe the callback could be provided the wait count, or even the CQE to wait for. Something I'll think on. It does seem more flexible than the simple wait count. It's also a nice reuse of the async helpers I PRed.

