Of course (same goes for the `result` in the same struct). 

> Why not provide it and set it when creating the asem struct? e.g.
> 
> ```c
> struct async_sem asem = {
>   .sem = &some_sem;
>   .context = &some_context;
> };
> ```
> 
> Because a CONTAINER_OF can do just as good of a job if an author puts the k_sem/k_mutex/k_work in a container struct to hold the added info. For k_work this is the documented method and I'm happy to document that here as well.

The intention of providing the context within the async struct is not to lose data in the callback chain. Sure, each application can store this struct within another struct, fill it in, call CONTAINER_OF when receiving the notification etc., but the whole point of introducing this feature is to standardize on one pattern and reduce application level boilerplate code. Having a standardized way of retrieving the context from a callback will help further reduce the amount of application level code needed to retrieve the same context.

If the context is not provided, and the application needs it, it would be simpler to not use the async helpers at all and just implement your own callback (which gives access to both context and result) and trigger the IPC from there than having to put structures withing structures just to get to what was thrown away in the async helper callback chain.

