While I think the virtual controller as a bridge is good for the device tree with binding. I think it would still be good to have the `i2c_driver_api` within the `i3c_driver_api` rather than reinventing the wheel here with new i2c function apis (and defines) within the i3c api. My concern is about maintainability. All i3c devices support i2c (to an extent..) and controllers should all support the existing i2c functionality within the zephyr i2c, as well as if any future defines or apis are added to the i2c layer, that will carry in to the i3c.  For example, I got to get an i3c controller working as an i2c target for me, and there already is an existing i2c api for targets (currently called slave). It would make sense for an i3c controller driver to just extend in to the `i2c_driver_api` for the target functions.
The only exception would be the i2c attaching function, `i3c_i2c_device_register`, for adding it into the slist required by i3c for bus mode and DEFTGTS which you've already got figured out...

It also would make the virtual layer much more straight forward with calling it's parent's i2c api functions. I also just can't see any of the existing i2c_driver_api (and defines) needed to be any different for an i3c controller
```
__subsystem struct i3c_driver_api {
