You could argue that `SYS_INIT` exists purely as an optimisation of `struct device` for use-cases where all the overhead of a `struct device` is not needed. This is the rationale behind 8d7bb8ffd8faba73628e18e4651e20896da9751e. Forcing anything that wants to expose itself as something that can be a dependency to go back to `struct device` would be a substantial regression. All to avoid making the name generated by `SYS_INIT` deterministic.

> Ideally because it generates it: The app says "Here is my device. Here are its dependencies. Here is its initialization function. Please call it at the appropriate time, kthx" Then the device layer build integration goes and makes that happen, presumably by emitting a bunch of SYS_INIT() expansions in generated code.

I still don't see it. In the following setup, what do you put in `some_device` to show that it depends on the filesystem?
```
SYS_INIT(filesystem_init);

DEVICE_DT_DEFINE(some_device, device_init, ..., ???);
SYS_INIT(filesystem_monitor, ???);
```
My answer is:
```
SYS_INIT(filesystem_init);

DEVICE_DT_DEFINE(some_device, device_init, ...,  SOME_MACRO(filesystem_init));
SYS_INIT(filesystem_monitor, SOME_MACRO(filesystem_init));
```
`SOME_MACRO` needs to be able to  generate a reference to the `SYS_INIT` structures for other tooling to process. That is what this PR will enable.

