I'm confused on the approach, what I recall from the TSC discussion was that if we want to standardize on some DSP API in Zephyr, it should be an architecture neutral solution supported and accepted by the different architectures stakeholders.

Here's some input from my DSP expert colleagues on the ARC dsplib solution. In general they agree to a CMSIS-DSP like/compatible API, that's what we did for the ARC dsplib as well. Here are some examples of that.
Features covered:

- Vector math (abs, add, sub, mult, dot_product, neg, offset, scale, min, max, sine, cosine, sqrt, inv-sqrt, exp, log2, ln, lg)
- Complex vector math (conjugate, dot_product, magnitude, magnitude_squared, compex_mul_complex, complex_mul_real)
- Matrix functions (add, sub, mult, scale, complex add/sub/mult/scale), transpose)
- "FIR like" kernels (real FIR, complex FIR, real convolution, correlation)
- Transforms (CFFT, iCFFT, RFFT, iRFFT)

For most cores the library is offered for a number of datatypes, depending on their support on the particular processor. Typically those include fixed-point (Q31, Q16 usually, sometimes Q7), floating point (f32).
Many functions are offered for complex and real data.

The API is straightforward, to give a few examples:

```
//vector dot product
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_dot_prod_f32(const __AGU f32_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU f32_t * __restrict pSrcB, uint32_t nSamples, f32_t *result);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_dot_prod_q31(const __AGU q31_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU q31_t * __restrict pSrcB, uint32_t nSamples, q63_t *result);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_dot_prod_q15(const __AGU q15_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU q15_t * __restrict pSrcB, uint32_t nSamples, q63_t *result);

//addition of 2 vectors
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_add_f32(const __AGU f32_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU f32_t * __restrict pSrcB, __AGU f32_t * __restrict pDst, uint32_t nSamples);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_add_q31(const __AGU q31_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU q31_t * __restrict pSrcB, __AGU q31_t * __restrict pDst, uint32_t nSamples);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_add_q15(const __AGU q15_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU q15_t * __restrict pSrcB, __AGU q15_t * __restrict pDst, uint32_t nSamples);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_add_q7(const __AGU q7_t * __restrict pSrcA, const __AGU q7_t * __restrict pSrcB, __AGU q7_t * __restrict pDst, uint32_t nSamples);

//inverse of square matrix [M x M] = [M x M]
dsp_status _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_mat_inverse_f32(const matrix_f32_t  *pSrc, matrix_f32_t  *pDst);

// Some flavors of FFT:
dsp_status _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_init_f32(cfft_f32_t *handle, uint32_t length, __AGU f32_t * pState);
dsp_status _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_init_q31(cfft_q31_t *handle, uint32_t length, __AGU uint32_t * pState);
dsp_status _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_init_q15(cfft_q15_t *handle, uint32_t length, __AGU uint32_t * pState);
void dsp_cfft_delete_f32(cfft_f32_t *handle);
void dsp_cfft_delete_q31(cfft_q31_t *handle);
void dsp_cfft_delete_q15(cfft_q15_t *handle);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_f32(const cfft_f32_t *handle, const __AGU f32_t * input, __AGU f32_t * output);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_q31(const cfft_q31_t *handle, const __AGU q31_t * input, __AGU q31_t * output);
void _DSPLIB_XYCC dsp_cfft_q15(const cfft_q15_t *handle, const __AGU q15_t * input, __AGU q15_t * output);
```

You see we use a neutral 'dsp' prefix, nothing architecture specific. And we have some extra type attributes/compiler hints to support ARC CPU special optimizations supported by the Metaware toolchain. These deal with special DSP XY memories (_DSPLIB_XYCC) and use of automatic load/store of operands ( __AGU).

Lets net overdue this, currently we have nodes for flash, partition, Kconfig for disk and Kconfig for file system, if there would be any put on top of the disk.
As an example, SoC flash definition from dts/arm/nordic/nrf52840.dtsi:
```
371                         flash0: flash@0 {
372                                 compatible = "soc-nv-flash";
373                                 erase-block-size = <4096>;
374                                 write-block-size = <4>;
375                         };
376                 };
```
Partition on this flash0:
```
254 &flash0 {
...
283                 /*
284                  * Storage partition will be used by FCB/LittleFS/NVS
285                  * if enabled.
286                  */
287                 storage_partition: partition@f8000 {
288                         label = "storage";
289                         reg = <0x000f8000 0x00008000>;
290                 };
291         };
```
Then Kconfigs for disk:
```
CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_DEV_NAME 
CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_START
CONFIG_DISK_ERASE_BLOCK_SIZE
CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_ERASE_ALIGNMENT 
CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_MAX_RW_SIZE
CONFIG_DISK_VOLUME_SIZE
```
and finally FAT FS configuration:
```
CONFIG_FS_FATFS_MAX_SS
```
Above values affect how is the data writes/reads/erases handled by subsystems; DTS entries affect disk access without placing system over it, Kconfig affect file system operation.

What DTS misses is read alignment and minimal size, which is characteristic of, at least, some external devices (look at `CONFIG_NORDIC_QSPI_NOR_STACK_WRITE_BUFFER_SIZE`).

FAT FS uses `CONFIG_FS_FATFS_MAX_SS` to set sector for read/write window, and this is MAX value so if system will mount devices with sectors that are 512 and 4096, this should be 4096. The `erase-block-size` value is used only when `mkfs` operation is performed and is written to FAT FS characteristics on a device. This is done by calling `disk_access_ioctl` with `DISK_IOCTL_GET_SECTOR_SIZE` command.

When dealing with moving disk definition to DTS we need to avoid taking over what is "software configuration" and adding redundant configuration items.

For disk placed on fixed-partition, we can take `CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_START` and `CONFIG_DISK_VOLUME_SIZE` from partition,
the `CONFIG_DISK_ERASE_BLOCK_SIZE` should be taken from controller and `CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_ERASE_ALIGNMENT` is redundant, as it can not be different than `CONFIG_DISK_ERASE_BLOCK_SIZE`; `CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_MAX_RW_SIZE` does not make sense and should be replaced with `write-alignment`, although this is problematic as in case of NOR devices this thing is actually configured in Kconfig, not DTS.

Ths means that `CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_START`, `CONFIG_DISK_VOLUME_SIZE`, `CONFIG_DISK_FLASH_ERASE_ALIGNMENT`, `CONFIG_DISK_ERASE_BLOCK_SIZE` can be already dropped, because they can be extracted as characteristics superimposed on flash disk, by nodes it derived from.

Whatever will be using flash disk should be given information on the raw device characteristic underneath and then device, as minimal required: minimal erase size, minimal write alignment, etc. Then the software may have modifiers (multipliers) to these values exposed by Kconfig, if it is needed.
For exmaple FAT FS Kconfig `CONFIG_FS_FATFS_MAX_SS` could be multiply of what flash device is providing as `erase-block-size`. 
~~Side note:  I also think that we may have bug here, because there is also MIN value for that (in ffconfg.h), and when they differ then FAT FS uses `disk_ioctl`  command  `DISK_IOCTL_GET_SECTOR_SIZE`  to get block size for a device at run-time, which allows to apply different (no bigger than MAX) block sizes for different devices, and we set both values to the same thing.~~ Verified, it is ok.


I think that this is maximum what disk entry should provide:
```
flash_disk: disk0 {
  compatible = "zephyr,flash_disk";
  partition = &storage;
};
```

