Just catching up on the conversation, but if I'm understanding correctly the core issue here is that we need a generic way to describe a region of flash that the flash disk driver should use. If that's the case, I generally agree with what @de-nordic has outlined in [this comment](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/50856#issuecomment-1273123756), but question if we need a `flash-disk` chosen node, or could use a flash partition directly.

With that we have a few potential restrictions, as @de-nordic mentioned:
- minimum write size
- minimum erase size

We also of course need to know where in the flash device this region resides, and the size (both can be encoded by a flash partition)

In the case of the flash driver, we have the [flash_get_parameters](https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/hardware/peripherals/flash.html#c.flash_get_parameters) API available, which would permit the flash disk driver to get the minimum write size. Since write size is queried via the [disk_access_ioctl](https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/services/storage/disk/access.html#c.disk_access_ioctl) API, we could get this parameter at runtime.

With that, could we do something like the following?
```
chosen {
    zephyr,storage-partition = &storage_partition;
};
...
&flash0 {
   partitions {
       ....
       storage_partition: partition@1e000 {
		label = "storage";
		reg = <0x0001e000 0x00002000>;
	};
   };
};
```

If we always use a partition within a flash device, then we can reference the parent flash controller in the `flashdisk.c` driver, and call `flash_get_parameters` in order to get properties like the minimum flash write size. As a bonus, this would offer a benefit to other samples (such as the littlefs one) that use a `storage_partition` directly by its nodelabel

