Assuming you have your DTS:
```
something {
    compatible = ""jedec,spi-nor";
    ...
}
```
running on some of your devices. Now you get your hands on faster MX device, that supports the hi perf mode, and you build the devices. Above DTS still works. You create new firmware with DTS:
```
something {
   compatible = ""jedec,spi-nor";
   mxicy,high-perf-mode = true;
   ...
}
```
Now you have two boards with compatible devices, old firmware will work on both, but will not use the feature. How to do that new firmware will work on both, assuming that you can accept lower performance mode?

Now you run out of the mx devices, because <reasons>, and you have to use similar25f64 device. This is SPI NOR device which is in place compatible with mx25 but will not support the hi perf mode.

Older firmware should work, due to mx25 compatibility, newer firmware also, but using m25 compatibility mode without the hi-perf mode.

Now you discover that there is similar-hi-perf-mode, that is using different init code than the mx one available in something25 devices, but it is broken and you want to have it disabled. Your new DTS looks like this:
```
something {
   compatible = ""jedec,spi-nor";
   mxicy,high-perf-mode = true;
   something,high-perf-mode = false;
  ...
}
```
Now can you issue single firmware that will cover all devices that differ by single IC on board?

What I propose is to have `mx_extensions/mx_specific_configuration` function called by `spi_nor_configure`, when there is any property added to NOR SPI compatible device, that is mx vendor) specific. Such function will test itself against jedec id from device and successfully exit when vendor does not match, otherwise it will attempt to enable features requested by set properties for that vendor; here it may issue warnings/errors in case of failure.
The same with something25 specific extensions, which we are not providing at this time.

We read device ID at configure so we are able to find out which extensions to run.

