@nordicjm, why not leave the `is_valid` also to be defined by the user. A user that has only one byte would insert some parity check, a user that has 2kB might insert one CRC32 or multiple crc8 or even a hash/multiple hashes,... whatever suits the data that is shared.

IMO the added value is in the easy interface to a area that can be shared by multiple application (e.g. a bootloader and a application) and making the interface simple: `read/write/erase` and `getsize` as well as making the definition simple (just declaring the area) will stimulate users to take advantage of this interface. Adding predetermined functionality will require extra checks in the definition (e.g. avoid definition of a prefix and a crc in a one byte area) that can be avoided.

There might be one exception when a hardware provided area requires the usage of a specific `validation`, but I don't know if there is such a thing.

Just for reference:

### Battery backed ram
```
__subsystem struct bbram_driver_api {
	bbram_api_check_invalid check_invalid;
	bbram_api_check_standby_power check_standby_power;
	bbram_api_check_power check_power;
	bbram_api_get_size get_size;
	bbram_api_read read;
	bbram_api_write write;
};
```
### EEPROM
```
__subsystem struct eeprom_driver_api {
	eeprom_api_read read;
	eeprom_api_write write;
	eeprom_api_size size;
};
```

These can easily be either wrapped or replaced by the API in this PR. We loose the `check_power` and `check_standby_power` from BBRAM, but note that only one of the 4 in-tree bbram drivers actually use those functions, the others use only `check_invalid` or none of them. Add a memset function to the API in this PR and we have a good contender :)

@semihalf-duda-patryk, @de-nordic, in order to get this api in line with the syscall requirements the following should work:
a. The additional functions are split up in a `get` and `set` method,
b. The `code` parameter is converted into a struct `io_code` that consists of a `code` and a `offset`,

So the interface would look like:
```
struct io_code {
        uint32_t code;
        offset offset;
};

int flash_ex_op_set(const struct device *dev, const struct io_code code, const void *data, size_t len);
int flash_ex_op_get(const struct device *dev, const struct io_code code, void *data, size_t len);
```

This somewhat limits the possibilities, but even the most complicated function I can think of should be possible with a combination of a set+get command. And it keeps the number of function parameters limited to 4.

