@arbrauns - that is correct - one step at a time though. First get the new RTC driver API in, some implementations including `rtc_emul`, and then modify POSIX (and libc) code to use the new Zephyr driver + API.

I would go so far as to say as well, that Zephyr should have an RTC present *by default* (and we do not normally default things to yes), even if it is simply the `rtc_emul` device. Furthermore, we should use Kconfig to have other parts of code depend on the presence of an RTC.

> It seems you don't believe me that setting all alarm bits to zero is a valid alarm. Here is a table from the rv3032 documentation. Maybe this will explain better why setting all the match bits to disabled is a valid configuration: ![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/35062987/226456423-65f97509-3e40-4e6b-82f6-0ce4cd6095eb.png)

The RTC API alarm time mask is not a 1-1 mapping with any specific RTC device register, it is a higher level abstraction.

If I want to disable the alarm, I will write the following:
```
/* Every bit in mask is 0 */
rtc_alarm_set_time(rtc, 0, 0, NULL);
```
Following the description of the API `The mask designates which fields of the RTC alarm time to enable. If the mask parameter is 0, the alarm will be disabled.` the RTC driver will not change the alarm enable bits in this instance, it will simply disable the alarm AE_0

If I want the to enable the alarm, I could write the following:
```
struct rtc_time alarm_time = {
    .tm_min = 30,
    .tm_hour = 11,
};

uint16_t alarm_mask = (RTC_ALARM_TIME_MASK_MINUTE
                    |  RTC_ALARM_TIME_MASK_HOUR);

rtc_alarm_set_time(rtc, 0, alarm_mask, &alarm_time);
```
In this case, the RTC device will set AE_0 to enable the alarm, and configure the alarm interrupt bits to match the alarm time mask
`AE_D = 1, AE_H=0, AE_M=0`

For this RTC device, it can support all mixes of MASK_MINUTE, MASK_HOUR and MASK_MONTHDAY, including all being 0.

