@iocapa Thanks for your effort on #56678 . As this PR is older and has already gone through some reviews, it will be merged first, as the bare minimum required for using PIO. You can then update your PR to add your new features to the existing implementation.
One suggestion that was integrated into this PR was to move the pinctrl to the child nodes rather than the parent PIO device, allowing multiple devices to be defined under one PIO device. The sample was updated to demonstrate a loopback on two UARTs running on a single PIO.
If any of your other changes would require breaking an API later, please say so now, so that I might fix it before this is merged.
@soburi your review would also be appreciated.

@yonsch 

> EDIT: I see that #56678 does not need to add `pinmux-cells`. However, in that manner the driver forces the pinctrl to be defined in a certain way. In the example above from the UART driver, it is assumed that the pinctrl is called `pio0_uart0_default`, and that the tx and rx pins are at index 0. For peripherals with more pins (e.g. SPI) that would mean that a specific ordering would be needed in the pinmux array. My point is - IMO both implementations use a custom pinctrl scheme

Yes, I don't see a way not to have a custom pinctrl scheme. 
The initial idea in #56678 was to force the user in defining 
the pins in a certain way since there's a 2nd stage pinmux at 
the PIO level, but still reusing existing infrastructure to 
make it look "similar" to other drivers.

`DT_RPI_PICO_PIO_PIN_BY_NAME` allows getting a `pinctrl-N` 
instance by name, indexing into it, getting a specific group 
by name and indexing into it's pinmux. It was made this way 
to not force anything on the user, other than documenting 
the approach taken. 

In the current scheme (`DT_PIO_PIN_BY_NAME`) you are 
pretty much required to name each and every pin. 
This will get pretty ugly for a high bit count parallel port, 
and you will still be constrained by PIO internals on how 
those pins could be assigned.

For example, looks like the current PR needs to define a 4 bit 
parallel port in the following way:
```
pio_bus_default: pio_bus_default {
		pio_bus: group1 {
			#pinmux-cells = <1>;
			pinmux = <PIO1_P2>, <PIO1_P3>, <PIO1_P4>, <PIO1_P5>;
			input-enable;
			bias-pull-up;
		};
	};
...
pinctrl-0 = <&pio_bus_default>;
pinctrl-names = "default";
pinmuxs = <&pio_bus 0>, <&pio_bus 1>, <&pio_bus 2>, <&pio_bus 3>;
pinmux-names = "d0", "d1", "d2", "d3";
```
However, you can't really change the order, since in the driver you will 
need the base, which will always be the lowest index (0, d0, <&pio_bus 0>), 
and any potential change needs to be made in 3 different places (pinmux, pinmuxs, pinmux-names).
```
uint8_t base = DT_INST_PIO_PIN_BY_NAME(inst, d0);
```

#56678 does the same thing, but directly at the `pinmux` level.
In this case, a potential change only needs to be made at the `pinmux` level.
```
pio_bus_default: pio_bus_default {
		pio_bus {
			pinmux = <PIO1_P2>, <PIO1_P3>, <PIO1_P4>, <PIO1_P5>;
			input-enable;
			bias-pull-up;
		};
	};
...
pinctrl-0 = <&pio_bus_default>;
pinctrl-names = "pinctrl_name";
```

and getting it by:
```
uint8_t base = DT_INST_RPI_PICO_PIO_PIN_BY_NAME(inst, pinctrl_name, pio_bus, 0);
```

Another thing that is unclear to me is how you would use `pinmuxs` and `pinmux-names` 
to implement more than 1 pinctrl handle. Looks like you need to add it to `pinmux-names` 
but with different names for the same pin.
```
pinctrl-0 = <&pio_bus_default>;
pinctrl-1 = <&pio_bus_extra>;
pinctrl-names = "default", "extra";
pinmuxs = <&pio_bus_default_pins 0>, <&pio_bus_default_pins 1>, <&pio_bus_extra_pins 0>, <&pio_bus_extra_pins 1>;
pinmux-names = "d0_default", "d1_default", "d0_extra", "d1_extra";
```

So IMO, a PIO based driver should document the pin allocation 
scheme requirements as there are some constraints, and the scheme should be decided
by the child driver in a way that makes sense for that specific driver.

