@PerMac The documentation on writing and running Robot Framework tests in Renode can be found in: https://renode.readthedocs.io/en/latest/introduction/testing.html. It provides a list of the most commonly used keywords together with links to the source code where those are defined.

For the list of keywords provided by the Robot Framework itself, refer to: https://robotframework.org/robotframework/.

Please also remember that it's possible to extend the framework by adding new keywords expressed directly in robot test suite files, as an external python library or, like Renode does it, dynamically via XML-RPC.

> How does this co-exist with `CONFIG_AARCH64_IMAGE_HEADER` and `CONFIG_ARM_ZIMAGE_HEADER`?

@carlocaione Currently this feature relies on ROM_START as an argument to zephyr_linker_sources, so it's limited to architectures supporting it. ARM64 is not supported yet, so it wouldn't work anyway.
I'm not familiar with ARM64 but from a glance it looks like that section could go after the image header and vector table, so it shouldn't be a problem.

@nashif what version of Renode do you use?

In https://github.com/renode/renode/commit/805006350fd1f8de9cd956a96bc0729ce590cbc8 we've added a support for automatic configuration of the Renode keywords source - you need at least this version to run robot tests (in a form proposed in this PR) in Renode.

FTR: This support is already included in Renode 1.13.3 that is officially used by Zephyr.

@trond-snekvik - Following up a little on this, I see that there is a pre-processor check that claims that this feature does not work properly with multiple CPUs. I am not sure if this is a make-or-break issue for you, but I thought that I would point it out.

If I am following things correctly, the reason for this limitation is the entry THREAD_INFO_OFFSET_K_CURR_THREAD--offset of the current thread. In an MP system, this entry corresponds only to the offset of the current thread on cpu[0]. Perhaps the list of offsets could be extended to indicate the size of the struct _cpu? This is new code to me, so I assume that there are subtleties about which I am unaware.

If you would like to file an enhancement request, it will ensure that this issue and potential solution is tracked.

