## Patch notes
### Moved GNSS dump functions
As suggested by @fabiobaltieri , A new library has been added to `drivers/gnss` which dumps the contents of the structs used for GNSS to strings. The `gnss_publish.c` library has been expanded to dump published GNSS data if the symbol `CONFIG_GNSS_LOG_DUMP` is selected. The library will also lay the foundation for GNSS shell commands when that is added in the future :)

The output is a bit less pretty than what was included with the sample, but it is now more generic, using a 1-line JSON like structure. Example output:
```
gnss: gnss_info: {satellites_cnt: 14, hdop: 0.850, fix_status: GNSS_FIX, fix_quality: GNSS_SPS}
gnss: navigation_data: {latitude: 57.162330799, longitude : 9.961097799, bearing 12.530, speed 0.87, altitude: 42.371}
gnss: gnss_time: {hour: 16, minute: 17, millisecond 41000, month_day 3, month: 10, century_year: 23}
gnss: gnss_satellite: {prn: 13, snr: 15, elevation 74, azimuth 158, system: GPS, is_tracked: 1}
gnss: gnss_satellite: {prn: 22, snr: 22, elevation 60, azimuth 124, system: GPS, is_tracked: 1}
```

### Simplified the sample
The sample now only registers the two GNSS callbacks, printing small hints regarding the behavior of any and all GNSS devices in the system. If a GNSS modem has a fix, it will print `<name of gnss device> has fix!`. If there are satellites reported, it prints `gnss reported <number of satellites> satellites!` The sample selects `CONFIG_GNSS_LOG_DUMP` as well, so the "still kinda pretty" dumps will also be logged :)
```
...
gnss: gnss_satellite: {prn: 1, snr: 30, elevation 71, azimuth 276, system: GLONASS, is_tracked: 1}
gnss: gnss_satellite: {prn: 11, snr: 30, elevation 62, azimuth 221, system: GLONASS, is_tracked: 1}
gnss reported 2 satellites!
...
```

### General cleanup
I looked through the different commits to ensure the changes are located correctly, and updated the sample README to reflect the new behavior and output of the sample application.

