Process WG notes -
@aescolar - Handling of modules is not uniform.  Current process has too much overhead.  Some modules don't follow it at all, some lag upstream with a big gap.
@aescolar - propose that modules with active upstream should be upstream first.  For modules that aren't active, we essentially have a fork in Zephyr.
@aescolar - this allows upstream maintainers to review changes and run CI.
@aescolar - what about forks for bug fixes?  But the upstream module should want the bug fix.  In some cases short lived branches may be needed, but these branches should be abandoned once the upstream fix lands.
@dleach02 - what modules run into these problems?  Nordiq does a resync of upstream changes into their managed fork.  For some of their modules, the upstream module is sometimes slow.  Nordiq uses a tagging system to manage the patches between upstream and local.
@nashif - This proposal is only looking at the perspective of the Zephyr module maintainer.  We're documenting the exceptions as the main flow.  Modules are not uniform on how they are managed.
@nashif - submitting changes to a module is difficult - each module has a different process.  It's hard to identify the correct branch to submit changes.
@nashif - as a contributor, identifying where to submit upstream can be a challenge for the PR author.
@nashif - Zephyr board has asked that we cleanup tracking and maintenance of modules. 
@nashif - goal should be identify rules that work on >80% of modules and use exceptions for the others.
@aescolar - Proposal is to simplify the process.  With an upstream first policy, push the bulk of the verification and testing to the upstream repository.  Zephyr's validation is limited to when we pin to a new version.
@aescolar agrees with most of the points from @nashif .  The documentation language can be tweaked.
@jfischer-no - do we tag modules with releases?
@dleach02 - adding tagging will ensure that sha references aren't lost.
@nashif - it would be nice to automate tagging, even outside of releases. 
@jfischer-no - dependencies between Zephyr and modules need to be tracked.
@aescolar - we're currently mirroring all modules, which protects us if the upstream project disappears.
@nashif - We need to identify the constraints for managing modules and how developers can make fixes.  Need to set the expectations for both maintainers and developers.
@nashif - developers are likely to submit a PR directly to the Zephyr fork.  If we require upstream first, then the developer has to then push the change upstream (3rd party - with a different process).
@nashif - urgent fixes need a place to live.   Creating a branch introduces problems - SHAs from branches aren't visible through Github.
@aescolar - disagrees that contributing to upstream modules is too complicated.  The upstream projects will have their own quirks but this can be managed.
@fabiobaltieri - maybe the module maintainer should help developers work with the upstream project.
@aescolar - if we have a fork, it is easier to contribute PRs quickly.  However, the fork doesn't have the right reviewers - upstream has the correct reviewers to ensure quality.
@nashif - there are different types of PRs needed - fixes specific to Zephyr and new features for the module.
@nashif - Zephyr modules provide the flexibility to manage local changes.  But most modules are just a mirror.
@nashif - current module handling is too variable, and the proposal doesn't provide enough constraints.
@aescolar - suggests creating a wiki for each module that describe the contribution process for that module.
@dleach02 - what's the problem we are trying to solve?    Agrees the documentation needs to be simplified.
@aescolar - current documentation only describes one way to maintain modules, but its not an ideal process, and the process isn't enforced.
@fabiobaltieri - The documented process and the actual implementation done by modules doesn't match.
@aescolar - proposal that the Zephyr repos track the same branch name as the upstream.  This replaces creating a Zephyr branch for the Zephyr project.
@nashif - how Zephyr specific patches handled?
@aescolar - need to fork to handle Zephyr specific patches.  The fork can be tagged with "zephyr_" name.  But this should be approved by the maintainers/release team.   The goal should be that the patch is migrated upstream and the fork then gets archived and the module switches back to main.
@dleach02 - Why can't we adopt the tagging workflow used by Nordiq.  @aescolar this process is useful when you have permanent changes in your local fork.  
@aescolar - most modules shouldn't need Zephyr specific patches.
@dleach02 - still likes the workflow of the Nordic tagging.  Acknowledges that if you're not merging frequently it can be hard to manage.
@nashif - there is quite a bit of agreement about modules, but we need to define the required pillars for managing modules.

