Process WG notes -

@nashif - The current requirements were added when the module concept was introduced. But we can't start over.  We need to build on top of the existing processes.
@aescolar - The documentation attempted to document a single mechanism for updating modules. 
@nashif - there is a bit variation between modules.  We need to define a general rule that is the guideline modules should try to achieve.
@aescolar - not attempting to remove any workflows, but document the preferred workflow for module maintenance.
@nashif - following upstream is not a point of contention
@nashif - developers need a path to submit fixes to modules. It's difficult for devs to know where the upstream project.  Maintainers of the module should help devs work through this process.
@jfischer-no - as a maintainer, it's not clear the correct path to fix issues.  Upstream workflows are all different.  This could be too much burden for the typical Zephyr developer.  Should the rules/procedures differ based on whether modules are required, optional, experimental?  
@aescolar - it's already an issue that some modules have an uncommon workflow.  @aescolar suggests creating a wiki page for each module that describes the process for contributing to the module.  Many modules are very active, and submitting upstream should be the main path.
@nashif - Upstream communities don't necessarily know of the Zephyr developers, so may be slow to pull in PRs submitting from Zephyr.
@nashif - what are the use cases?  Let's try to categorize modules into a few categories, and document the process for each category.  But there can be still some flexibility.
@nashif - currently reviewing modules.
@keith-zephyr - what are the proposed categories?  
@nashif - 1. things that only exist in Zephyr (no upstream).  Some HALs are not open from the vendor, so the module has no effective upstream.  Sometimes these are distributed as a targball downstream.
2. Tooling - native_sim, and things used by CI but not part of final images
3. Full on open source projects with an active community - mcuboot, etc.  We should follow these upstream.
4. Libraries - things that have specific changes to work with Zephyr APIs.  These modules with a Zephyr fork provide a buffer between upstream churn and the Zephyr project.
@dleach02 - NXP has a complicated HAL constructed multiple business units. If bugs are found the HAL, NXP accepts the PR but then also works to move the fix into the upstream SDK project (which has a 6 month release cadence).  @aescolar - this module requires a continuous fork.   @dleach02 - the NXP HAL only exists for Zephyr - there is no upstream equivalent.
@nashif - Microchip HAL is actually forked from the Zephyr module, so this may be another category
@MaureenHelm - Why are some HALs/modules forked and some are not?  When there isn't a fork, the developer has to maintain copies of both upstream and downstream.  When it's not a true GitHub fork, the PR has to be submitted twice.
@nashif - hal_intel is forked from intel/hal-intel.  It makes it easy to just "sync fork" in the Zephyr/hal_intel module.
@nashif - anything that has a true upstream and is maintained, these modules should have true fork relationship.
@aescolar - If you fork, the upstream admin ends up being an admin of both the upstream and downstream fork.
@MaureenHelm - this relationship is true for private forks only, not public forks.
@nashif - partial audit has uncovered some stale/unused modules.  Working to complete audit following the 3.7 release.  Need to identify if there are still use cases for each module.
@fabiobaltieri shared a link for creating forks between GitHub projects: https://support.github.com/request/fork
@keith-zephyr - for next steps, can we document the broad module categories as captured above?  We can then work to define the recommended process for each category.

