> That would work, indeed. Though "supported maybe" sounds a bit too vague. What about "support available" vs "support enabled" (as when node is "okay") ?

@erwango I was deliberately cautious about using a term along the lines of "available" -- for all the nodes that are not "okay", finding a matching compatible is IMO not enough to automatically assume support is "available". For example, the author of the devicetree may have added a node to document the configuration of a particular device while they were at it and deep in data sheets, even set the proper compatible, but may not have enabled the node just yet due to e.g. a driver limitation. Does that make sense? As discussed, I still would like to indicate that the device *may* actually work if enabled, but the "may" really needs to be conveyed.

