I cannot speak for mr @kartben but I've ran a test just now to check Gurubase. As my current work with zephyr is tied with EDF, I asked it "How to set a thread deadline in Zephyr?" and the result was not correct, although the answer was actually a change I'd love to see in the API for the future. According to Gurubase, this is how you set a deadline:
```c
// API:
void k_thread_deadline_set(struct k_thread *thread, k_timeout_t deadline);

// usage:
k_thread_deadline_set(my_thread_id, K_MSEC(1000));
```

However, currently the deadline must be passed in [hardware cycles](https://docs.zephyrproject.org/latest/doxygen/html/group__thread__apis.html#gad887f16c1dd6f3247682a83beb22d1ce):

```c
// API:
void k_thread_deadline_set(struct k_thread *thread, int deadline);

// usage:
k_thread_deadline_set(my_thread_id, 10000);
```

I don't really see the point of passing hardware cycles as the deadline when the rest of the API is tailored for the `k_timeout_t` type. Not only it's an inconsistency for the API - a deadline in cycles instead of ticks also doesn't seem to cause any major benefits within the kernel aside from reducing the chances of threads being of the same exact priority. It's also less portable and might overflow much quicker.

So in short, I agree with the answer provided by Gurubase - it's what I'd like to see for the EDF API. But it's not what we have yet, so it's an inaccurate answer.

