@stephanosio Yes, the issue here is that many users are still in Ubuntu 22.04 LTS (ships with python3.10), or Debian (latest==Bookworm with 3.11), or... So for those we have suddenly broken things.

As time passes, more users will be in newer distros' releases, so things will work by default for them even if we require a newer python release. And, now we also know to provide them updated documentation that guides them if not. Which we can do calmly after the release. We do not have any pressing need to increase the requirement beyond 3.10. 

But we have had quite a few people report that we broke things for them and that our docs guide them to a not working setup. Reverting by now seems like a quite reasonable approach to me.

